e to the current lab data. The murmur is more consistent with benign causes."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the presence of hypercalcemia (Ca2+ 10.9 mEq/L) and constitutional symptoms (weight loss, fatigue) argue for a plasma cell disorder (multiple myeloma) as the primary diagnosis under B4?", "summary": "Hypercalcemia and constitutional symptoms (weight loss) provide weak evidence for plasma cell disorders (B4), but the normal differential argues against lymphoma. This weakly supports B4 while weakly reducing B5."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the recent cold and reactive leukocytosis (elevated LAP) argue for a leukemoid reaction (B5) over malignant processes like CML or MPN?", "summary": "The recent viral illness and elevated LAP strongly favor reactive leukocytosis (B5) over chronic myeloproliferative disorders (B3). This moderately supports B5 and moderately reduces B3."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the elevated leukocyte alkaline phosphatase (LAP) and absence of blast counts in the differential argue against chronic myeloid leukemia (CML) within B3 in favor of reactive leukocytosis?", "summary": "Elevated LAP and absence of blasts are classic findings against CML (B3), but do not strongly affect other MPNs. This moderately reduces B3 and is neutral for B4/B5."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the normal leukocyte differential (no blasts) argue against a lymphoproliferative disorder (e.g., lymphoma) within B4 in favor of a non-malignant process?", "summary": "Normal differential reduces likelihood of lymphoma (B4) but does not exclude plasma cell disorders like multiple myeloma. Weakly against B4 and neutral for B3/B5."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the presence of unintentional weight loss and hypercalcemia argue against a leukemoid reaction (B5) as a benign process?", "summary": "Weight loss and hypercalcemia are atypical for leukemo